author: "Abhiuday Gupta"
---

Agent Nerves is the nervous system of Autonomic AI — a `NATS JetStream` bridge that every organ connects to for asynchronous communication. Organs do not call each other directly. They publish events and subscribe to streams.

## Architecture

Nerves initializes a set of preconfigured `JetStream` streams at startup. Each stream maps to an event category: `workflow.*`, `memory.*`, `execution.*`, `security.*`, `visual.*`, `system.*`. Events are `JSON`-typed with a schema version field. Subscribers filter by stream and subject pattern using `NATS`'s hierarchical wildcard syntax (`workflow.started`, `workflow.>.error`).

Every event carries a versioned schema:

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "evt_abc123",
  "source": "spine",
  "subject": "workflow.node.completed",
  "timestamp": "2026-06-21T10:00:00Z",
  "data": {
    "pipeline": "security-fix-pr",
    "node": "apply_patch",
    "status": "success",
    "duration_ms": 2340
  }
}
```

The bridge mode means Nerves is a thin layer over `NATS` — it handles stream bootstrapping, `NATS` connection lifecycle, and graceful reconnection. It does not persist events beyond what `JetStream`'s retention policy defines. It does not transform event payloads.

Streams are configured at startup from a `YAML` manifest:

```yaml
streams:
  - name: workflow_events
    subjects: ["workflow.>"]
    storage: file
    retention: limits
    max_age: 168h
    max_msgs: 100000

  - name: system_health
    subjects: ["system.>"]
    storage: memory
    retention: interest
    max_msgs: 1000
```

## Standalone Mode

`agent-nerves ping` checks `NATS` connectivity and returns server version, cluster status, and stream counts. `agent-nerves status` lists every connected client by organ ID, with last-seen timestamps. These are useful for debugging connectivity issues without running the full ecosystem.

`agent-nerves stream ls` lists all active streams and their current message counts. `agent-nerves stream view <name>` tails recent messages from a stream.

## Integrated Mode

In a full deployment, every organ connects to Nerves on startup. Spine publishes `workflow.started`, `workflow.node.completed`, and `workflow.failed` events. Brain publishes `memory.updated` on fact writes. Muscle publishes `execution.started` and `execution.completed`. Immune publishes `security.scan.completed`. Eyes publishes `visual.diff.ready`.

Consumers subscribe to relevant streams. The Mission Control dashboard subscribes to all `workflow.*` and `system.*` events for real-time `UI` updates. Heart subscribes to `system.health.*` for organ status tracking. The reconnection strategy uses exponential backoff with jitter: 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, capped at 30s — ensuring the system recovers quickly from transient `NATS` outages without hammering the server.

## Design Decisions

We chose `NATS JetStream` over `RabbitMQ`, `Redis Streams`, and `Kafka`. `RabbitMQ` was eliminated because its queue-based model requires explicit binding configuration for every consumer — too much ceremony for a dynamic organ fleet. `Kafka` was eliminated for the opposite reason: it is over-provisioned for our throughput (hundreds of events per second, not millions). `JetStream` offers `Kafka`-like persistence with `NATS`'s operational simplicity — a single binary, no `Zookeeper`, no partitioning complexity.

The typed event schema with versioning was added after an early incident where a schema change in `workflow.node.completed` silently broke the Mission Control dashboard. Now every event carries a `version` field, and consumers check compatibility on subscription.
